WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.828
This briefing is Book 10 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:04.828 --> 00:00:06.900
Cloud Boundary and Landing Zone.

4
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.668
Its argument is simple: the authorization
boundary, not the product catalog,

5
00:00:11.668 --> 00:00:15.225
decides which cloud features
an agency can actually use.

6
00:00:15.225 --> 00:00:17.037
We'll take four questions in turn.

7
00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:19.576
Why does the boundary
decide what you can use?

8
00:00:19.576 --> 00:00:20.825
What has to change?

9
00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:22.225
Who owns the boundary?

10
00:00:22.225 --> 00:00:23.413
And how do you get there?

11
00:00:23.413 --> 00:00:28.347
If you remember one thing, make it this:
treat the boundary as a list you can read,

12
00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:33.513
not a perimeter you can only accept, and
evidence every choice in your own package.

13
00:00:33.513 --> 00:00:38.675
The slides and notes carry every detail
and citation; I'll stick to what matters.

14
00:00:38.675 --> 00:00:41.390
Let's start with why the boundary decides.

15
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.015
Here's why the boundary
matters, in one example.

16
00:00:48.015 --> 00:00:52.353
Two agencies hold the same
Microsoft 365 license.

17
00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:55.935
One runs a commercial tenant
and gets network assessments,

18
00:00:55.935 --> 00:00:58.128
smarter routing and Adoption Score.

19
00:00:58.128 --> 00:01:00.727
The other runs GCC and gets none of them,

20
00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:04.789
because Microsoft documents
each one as unavailable there.

21
00:01:04.789 --> 00:01:06.902
Same license, different product.

22
00:01:06.902 --> 00:01:10.727
That happens because three parties
make three separate decisions.

23
00:01:10.727 --> 00:01:15.490
FedRAMP certifies a specific offering,
and the Marketplace is the record.

24
00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.014
The provider publishes,
feature by feature,

25
00:01:18.014 --> 00:01:20.678
what each government
environment leaves out.

26
00:01:20.678 --> 00:01:23.065
And the agency authorizes its own system,

27
00:01:23.065 --> 00:01:28.052
with its authorizing official accepting the
risk of what it configures and connects.

28
00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:30.527
What you can use is
where all three overlap.

29
00:01:30.527 --> 00:01:33.541
So a feature with no
citation isn't available.

30
00:01:33.541 --> 00:01:34.815
It's unverified.

31
00:01:34.815 --> 00:01:38.120
Next, why that gap stays
hidden for so long.

32
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.845
So why don't people see the gap sooner?

33
00:01:43.845 --> 00:01:46.832
Because it's invisible until
a design depends on it.

34
00:01:46.832 --> 00:01:53.096
The surfaces are shared: Intune in GCC is
the same instance as commercial Intune.

35
00:01:53.096 --> 00:01:56.807
The exclusions are scattered,
one sentence per feature page.

36
00:01:56.807 --> 00:01:59.008
And absence doesn't announce itself.

37
00:01:59.008 --> 00:02:03.208
A detection whose input never arrives
looks exactly like a quiet day.

38
00:02:03.208 --> 00:02:06.249
The book then tests a
widely repeated example,

39
00:02:06.249 --> 00:02:09.531
that five features are
silently missing from GCC.

40
00:02:09.531 --> 00:02:13.214
Checked against Microsoft's
documentation, only one is:

41
00:02:13.214 --> 00:02:17.457
the Locations view of network
connectivity in the admin center.

42
00:02:17.457 --> 00:02:21.078
The others turn out to be
GCC High and DoD gaps,

43
00:02:21.078 --> 00:02:25.383
not documented gaps at all,
or inside Azure's own scope.

44
00:02:25.383 --> 00:02:28.732
The lesson: an unverified
boundary story sends an

45
00:02:28.732 --> 00:02:32.082
agency off to build
compensations it doesn't need.

46
00:02:32.082 --> 00:02:35.300
Now, what actually
changes across a boundary.

47
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:42.750
To reason about any boundary,
the book uses its own model.

48
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.663
A boundary is any surface across
which four things change.

49
00:02:46.663 --> 00:02:50.149
Telemetry: which signals
actually arrive on your side.

50
00:02:50.149 --> 00:02:55.345
Identity: who a claim's issuer resolves
to, and GCC pairs with Entra ID

51
00:02:55.345 --> 00:03:00.725
in commercial Azure, while GCC High
and DoD pair with Azure Government.

52
00:03:00.725 --> 00:03:03.807
Enforcement: who has to enforce a control,

53
00:03:03.807 --> 00:03:07.887
which in GCC often means the
agency's own measurements.

54
00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:12.424
And provenance: whether a claim is
still authoritative, or only derived.

55
00:03:12.424 --> 00:03:14.992
Each of these raises
its own kind of drift,

56
00:03:14.992 --> 00:03:19.975
and detection for them is at different
stages; the slide shows where each stands.

57
00:03:19.975 --> 00:03:21.925
One more point worth holding onto.

58
00:03:21.925 --> 00:03:24.717
FedRAMP's definition of
federal customer data

59
00:03:24.717 --> 00:03:27.438
excludes the telemetry
a provider generates.

60
00:03:27.438 --> 00:03:29.951
No certification promises you a signal.

61
00:03:29.951 --> 00:03:32.480
Next, what changed at FedRAMP.

62
00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.918
FedRAMP itself changed in 2026,
and three changes matter here.

63
00:03:40.918 --> 00:03:44.530
First, services are now certified
in classes, A through D.

64
00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.557
Class C covers today's Moderate baseline
and Class D today's High, and agencies

65
00:03:49.557 --> 00:03:54.505
can still authorize at whatever security
category they judge appropriate.

66
00:03:54.505 --> 00:03:58.768
Second, the new rules have no provider
authorization boundary at all.

67
00:03:58.768 --> 00:04:02.748
The phrase doesn't appear once
in the machine-readable ruleset,

68
00:04:02.748 --> 00:04:05.504
and security category
may vary by resource.

69
00:04:05.504 --> 00:04:08.593
Third, change is notified
rather than re-approved,

70
00:04:08.593 --> 00:04:11.530
and the deadlines are
counted in business days.

71
00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.570
Routine changes need no notice;

72
00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:17.493
bigger ones have set notice
windows, and the slide lists them.

73
00:04:17.493 --> 00:04:23.903
Two dates are worth writing down:
adoption is mandatory on January 1,

74
00:04:23.903 --> 00:04:28.254
2027, and new Rev5 requests
stop on June 11, 2027.

75
00:04:28.254 --> 00:04:31.420
Now let's read what GCC actually is.

76
00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:39.570
Chapter 04 reads GCC from
Microsoft's own documents.

77
00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:44.670
GCC is the worldwide service, minus
a published list of exclusions.

78
00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.470
And that list isn't a ladder.

79
00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:52.709
Some features, like network connectivity
in the admin center and Adoption Score,

80
00:04:52.709 --> 00:04:55.782
are missing in GCC,
GCC High and DoD alike.

81
00:04:55.782 --> 00:04:58.433
Others reach GCC and stop there.

82
00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:03.175
The biggest documented loss is the
network connectivity layer: a daily score

83
00:05:03.175 --> 00:05:07.844
for each location, with performance
attributed to named network providers.

84
00:05:07.844 --> 00:05:11.682
A GCC agency has to fund
its own measurement instead.

85
00:05:11.682 --> 00:05:16.321
On the Marketplace, the GCC package
is certified at Class C, Moderate,

86
00:05:16.321 --> 00:05:20.545
and a second package with the same
name is in process at Class D.

87
00:05:20.545 --> 00:05:25.795
But GCC High is already Class D, and
it still lacks network connectivity.

88
00:05:25.795 --> 00:05:29.083
A higher class doesn't bring
back an excluded feature.

89
00:05:29.083 --> 00:05:31.220
Next, the landing zone.

90
00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:39.645
The landing zone is where the boundary
stops being a diagram and becomes policy.

91
00:05:39.645 --> 00:05:41.649
Each workload gets a subscription,

92
00:05:41.649 --> 00:05:44.620
placed in a management group
that carries its rules.

93
00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.843
The baseline hierarchy puts an
intermediate root under the tenant root,

94
00:05:48.843 --> 00:05:51.095
and that's where boundary policy lives.

95
00:05:51.095 --> 00:05:55.344
Platform groups hold shared services
like security and connectivity;

96
00:05:55.344 --> 00:05:57.720
landing zone groups hold the workloads.

97
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.082
Make Sandbox the default, so no new
subscription lands outside the boundary policy.

98
00:06:03.082 --> 00:06:07.602
Each boundary decision then gets a
mechanism, from allowed locations and

99
00:06:07.602 --> 00:06:12.196
resource types to exemptions that carry
an expiry date; the slide maps them.

100
00:06:12.196 --> 00:06:15.282
Roll every policy out in audit mode first.

101
00:06:15.282 --> 00:06:19.852
One catch: Microsoft's accelerators
are supported only in public Azure,

102
00:06:19.852 --> 00:06:23.957
so in Azure Government the platform
team adapts the code by hand.

103
00:06:23.957 --> 00:06:26.080
Next, the address plan.

104
00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:34.255
Chapter 06 makes the address plan
the join key for the evidence.

105
00:06:34.255 --> 00:06:38.305
A scan tells you what answered; the
IPAM tells you what was allocated.

106
00:06:38.305 --> 00:06:41.815
So every inventory reconciles against it,

107
00:06:41.815 --> 00:06:47.430
and BOD 23-01 already asks for
automated discovery every seven days.

108
00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:51.005
In Azure, the enterprise
IPAM stays the authority.

109
00:06:51.005 --> 00:06:55.859
Reserve the block there first, mirror
it into Virtual Network Manager pools,

110
00:06:55.859 --> 00:07:00.096
then use policy to deny any virtual
network without an allocation,

111
00:07:00.096 --> 00:07:01.868
and reconcile by discovery.

112
00:07:01.868 --> 00:07:06.410
Virtual Network Manager isn't named
in Microsoft's FedRAMP audit scope,

113
00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:10.029
so record it as a dependency
until coverage is confirmed.

114
00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:15.430
The book builds all this as layered modules,
each writing its state as evidence.

115
00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:18.408
And it treats outside inputs as code too,

116
00:07:18.408 --> 00:07:22.830
like the monthly Microsoft
endpoint list a GCC tenant uses.

117
00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.780
Next, the front door.

118
00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:32.068
Code anyone can apply from a
laptop isn't change control.

119
00:07:32.068 --> 00:07:36.994
So Chapter 07 adds a front door: a
governed request into the landing zone,

120
00:07:36.994 --> 00:07:40.167
which Microsoft calls
subscription vending.

121
00:07:40.167 --> 00:07:46.280
The loop runs from request, through approval,
plan, apply and reconcile, to close.

122
00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.941
The requester can't
approve their own request,

123
00:07:48.941 --> 00:07:52.793
and the approver reviews the actual
plan before anything changes.

124
00:07:52.793 --> 00:07:55.467
The reconcile step
carries the most weight.

125
00:07:55.467 --> 00:08:00.149
The pipeline writes the address
allocation back to the enterprise IPAM,

126
00:08:00.149 --> 00:08:02.413
and fails the run if the two disagree,

127
00:08:02.413 --> 00:08:06.943
because an unreconciled block is
shadow inventory the moment it exists.

128
00:08:06.943 --> 00:08:09.955
The pipeline sits inside
the boundary question too.

129
00:08:09.955 --> 00:08:12.184
Terraform state is sensitive data,

130
00:08:12.184 --> 00:08:15.567
and not every pipeline
service is on the Marketplace.

131
00:08:15.567 --> 00:08:18.011
So the default is a self-hosted runner,

132
00:08:18.011 --> 00:08:20.929
with state kept inside
the agency's boundary.

133
00:08:20.929 --> 00:08:23.070
Next, who owns all this.

134
00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.170
Who owns the boundary?

135
00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.382
The rule is one decider per row.

136
00:08:30.382 --> 00:08:34.761
The authorizing official determines
the boundary, signs every exception,

137
00:08:34.761 --> 00:08:37.869
and decides whether a change
needs reauthorization.

138
00:08:37.869 --> 00:08:40.287
That risk acceptance can't be delegated,

139
00:08:40.287 --> 00:08:44.095
and in federal agencies it's held
by government personnel only.

140
00:08:44.095 --> 00:08:46.520
The system owner is the boundary owner.

141
00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.350
They keep the boundary
record and the package,

142
00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.882
and they decide where a new workload goes.

143
00:08:51.882 --> 00:08:55.792
That matters, because vending an
unrecorded subscription is the

144
00:08:55.792 --> 00:08:59.846
cheapest way to turn an authorized
system into an unauthorized one.

145
00:08:59.846 --> 00:09:02.278
The CISO sets control requirements,

146
00:09:02.278 --> 00:09:06.358
and the ISSO reads provider change
notices against the record.

147
00:09:06.358 --> 00:09:11.607
The platform team builds everything, but
doesn't decide whether a gap is acceptable.

148
00:09:11.607 --> 00:09:14.265
And the contract is a
boundary decision too:

149
00:09:14.265 --> 00:09:17.882
only a contracting officer
changes what the agency bought.

150
00:09:17.882 --> 00:09:20.280
Next, handling what's outside.

151
00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:27.205
So what do you do with a capability
that's outside the boundary?

152
00:09:27.205 --> 00:09:31.037
NIST gives four options:
compensate, accept more risk,

153
00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:35.305
choose a more trustworthy
provider, or don't use the service.

154
00:09:35.305 --> 00:09:38.475
The work starts with a
register, one feature per row,

155
00:09:38.475 --> 00:09:41.042
each tied to the provider's own statement.

156
00:09:41.042 --> 00:09:45.480
Only vendor-confirmed rows support a
claim to a provider or an assessor.

157
00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.090
Then probe what actually
works in your tenant,

158
00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:51.455
on a schedule, testing for the
output rather than the switch.

159
00:09:51.455 --> 00:09:56.343
Where a gap is real, build an in-boundary
equivalent and say it's derived.

160
00:09:56.343 --> 00:10:01.392
Or take an exception, where the authorizing
official signs a dated risk acceptance.

161
00:10:01.392 --> 00:10:05.406
And one practical warning: buy
the capability, not the badge.

162
00:10:05.406 --> 00:10:10.618
Microsoft's Network Provider Program,
for example, is closed to new providers.

163
00:10:10.618 --> 00:10:12.500
Next, a third path.

164
00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:19.859
The third answer is an alternative
path: move the workload to a

165
00:10:19.859 --> 00:10:23.963
different certified service, and
leave the identity plane where it is.

166
00:10:23.963 --> 00:10:28.440
Google Workspace and Gemini for Government
are separate listings at different

167
00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.979
classes, so which AI
features sit inside which

168
00:10:30.979 --> 00:10:33.451
certification is a
question for the package.

169
00:10:33.451 --> 00:10:35.800
The public record shows both outcomes.

170
00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:39.245
One cabinet-level
department moved more than

171
00:10:39.245 --> 00:10:42.600
50,000 employees to
Google Workspace in 2025.

172
00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.499
A military department went the other way,

173
00:10:45.499 --> 00:10:50.700
ending about 150,000 Google email
licenses after an audit found few in use.

174
00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:54.225
Whichever way you go, keep
Entra ID as the source.

175
00:10:54.225 --> 00:10:58.712
Provision users, federate sign-in
and apply Conditional Access there.

176
00:10:58.712 --> 00:11:03.338
Then close the gaps: Google super
administrators sign in outside it,

177
00:11:03.338 --> 00:11:06.725
and Google's directory is
derived, so report drift.

178
00:11:06.725 --> 00:11:08.750
Next, the evidence.

179
00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:16.537
Finally, the boundary has to show
up in the agency's own package.

180
00:11:16.537 --> 00:11:19.686
The agency never authorizes
a cloud service;

181
00:11:19.686 --> 00:11:22.663
it authorizes its own
system that uses one.

182
00:11:22.663 --> 00:11:26.794
So its plan should hold only what
the agency is responsible for,

183
00:11:26.794 --> 00:11:31.163
and FedRAMP is blunt: don't copy
and paste provider controls into it.

184
00:11:31.163 --> 00:11:35.892
Instead, write the boundary down once,
as a record the system owner keeps,

185
00:11:35.892 --> 00:11:40.542
and cite it everywhere: the system
description, each external service,

186
00:11:40.542 --> 00:11:44.150
each crossing, the POA&M and
the component definitions.

187
00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:46.262
Know whose package you're reading, too.

188
00:11:46.262 --> 00:11:48.174
GCC runs on Azure Government,

189
00:11:48.174 --> 00:11:52.237
so the inheritance chain runs
through two provider packages.

190
00:11:52.237 --> 00:11:57.384
And a compensating narrative takes just
four sentences: what the control needs,

191
00:11:57.384 --> 00:12:02.375
what's unavailable, what runs instead,
and where the remaining gap is recorded.

192
00:12:02.375 --> 00:12:05.170
Next, what to ask your provider for.

193
00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:12.845
Appendix A turns all of this
into requests to the provider.

194
00:12:12.845 --> 00:12:17.059
Three habits make a request
hard to deflect: name one item,

195
00:12:17.059 --> 00:12:21.183
cite the basis with a link, and
ask for data, not documents.

196
00:12:21.183 --> 00:12:24.544
The book makes eight requests,
and the slide lists them.

197
00:12:24.544 --> 00:12:29.498
One number shows why trust centers
made the list: when the book checked,

198
00:12:29.498 --> 00:12:33.852
only 110 of 781 Marketplace
listings had a trust-center entry,

199
00:12:33.852 --> 00:12:37.695
and none of the Microsoft
365 government packages did.

200
00:12:37.695 --> 00:12:43.057
Five of the eight requests rest on obligations
the rules already place on providers.

201
00:12:43.057 --> 00:12:47.183
And if the answer is no, a
documented no is evidence too.

202
00:12:47.183 --> 00:12:49.370
Let's close with the decisions.

203
00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:54.495
So, four decisions.

204
00:12:54.495 --> 00:12:57.624
Build the feature register
on cited rows only,

205
00:12:57.624 --> 00:13:01.083
and drop or relabel anything
without a vendor page.

206
00:13:01.083 --> 00:13:05.248
Name the boundary owner: the authorizing
official decides the boundary,

207
00:13:05.248 --> 00:13:09.344
and the system owner keeps the record
and gates every new subscription.

208
00:13:09.344 --> 00:13:14.149
Enforce the boundary in the landing zone,
with policy at the intermediate root,

209
00:13:14.149 --> 00:13:16.957
audit before deny, and
the governed front door.

210
00:13:16.957 --> 00:13:20.653
And evidence every response,
whether it's an equivalent,

211
00:13:20.653 --> 00:13:24.832
an exception or an alternative
path, in the agency's own package.

212
00:13:24.832 --> 00:13:27.209
Before your next architecture review,

213
00:13:27.209 --> 00:13:31.220
list every cloud feature your
designs depend on, with its package,

214
00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:35.082
its availability in your environment,
and the page that says so.

215
00:13:35.082 --> 00:13:38.045
Then put Appendix A's
requests to your provider.

216
00:13:38.045 --> 00:13:42.045
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

217
00:13:42.045 --> 00:13:45.570
Book 10 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

218
00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:46.400
Thank you.
